      <div purpose="section" id="migration">
        <div purpose="section-with-image">
          <div purpose="feature-text">
            <h3>How the move works</h3>
            <p>Most teams assume switching means wiping every laptop and starting over in ABM. It doesn’t. A Fleet engineer maps your Jamf profiles, policies, scripts, and packages to Fleet first. Then you point your devices at Fleet in ABM, and Apple’s native MDM migration moves each Mac in place. Thumbtack moved more than 90% of its Macs with no help from IT. Migration support comes with the license.</p>
            <div purpose="section-buttons">
              <a purpose="cta-button" class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact?talkToUs">Get a demo</a>
              <animated-arrow-button href="/case-study/thumbtack">Read Thumbtack’s story</animated-arrow-button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div purpose="section-image">
            <img alt="Device health checks passing across Macs after migration" src="/images/what-stays-what-goes-800x600@2x.png">
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div purpose="page-section">
        <div purpose="comparison">
          <div purpose="comparison-intro">
            <h2>Fleet vs. Jamf</h2>
            <p>Jamf built a real community around Mac admins, and it earned its place as the default. Here is where the two differ.</p>
          </div>
          <div purpose="comparison-grid">
            <div purpose="comparison-head">
              <span>Capability</span>
              <span>Fleet</span>
              <span>Jamf</span>
            </div>
            <% [
              { capability: 'Apple MDM protocol, DDM, and Apple Business Manager (ABM)',
                fleet: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Supported' },
                jamf:  { mark: 'yes', text: 'Supported' } },
              { capability: 'Windows and Linux in the same place',
                fleet: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Supported' },
                jamf:  { mark: 'no', text: 'Not supported' } },
              { capability: 'Answering a question about a Mac right now',
                fleet: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Ask any device, answer in seconds' },
                jamf:  { mark: 'part', text: 'Inventory on a check-in cycle' } },
              { capability: 'Vulnerability detection and CIS benchmarks',
                fleet: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Included in the license' },
                jamf:  { mark: 'part', text: 'Requires Jamf Protect' } },
              { capability: 'Self-hosting',
                fleet: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Same features as Fleet-hosted' },
                jamf:  { mark: 'part', text: 'Some features, like DDM, are cloud-only' } },
              { capability: 'Open source',
                fleet: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Public' },
                jamf:  { mark: 'no', text: 'Proprietary' } },
            ].forEach((row)=>{ %>
            <div purpose="comparison-row">
              <div purpose="comparison-capability"><%= row.capability %></div>
              <% [{key: 'fleet', label: 'Fleet'}, {key: 'jamf', label: 'Jamf'}].forEach((product)=>{ var cell = row[product.key]; %>
              <div purpose="comparison-cell" class="<%= cell.mark %>">
                <span purpose="comparison-label"><%= product.label %></span>
                <span purpose="comparison-value"><%= cell.text %></span>
              </div>
              <% }); %>
            </div>
            <% }); %>
          </div>
          <p purpose="comparison-note"><a href="/compare/jamf-vs-fleet#key-differences">See the full comparison</a>.</p>
          <div purpose="callout">
            <div purpose="callout-text">
              <h3>None of this is unusual, and you don't have to fix it alone</h3>
              <p>We'll do the migration for you. Talk to someone who has moved large Mac fleets off Jamf.</p>
            </div>
            <a purpose="cta-button" class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact?talkToUs">Get a demo</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div purpose="features-section">
        <div purpose="section-heading">
          <h2>What you get with Fleet</h2>
          <p>Everything you use Jamf for today, plus the parts it charges extra for, on every operating system you run.</p>
        </div>
        <div purpose="feature-with-image-row">
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <div purpose="feature-image">
              <img alt="AI agents and coding assistants installed on a Mac" src="/images/endpoint-governance-hero-1072x520@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="image-caption">
              <h5>See which AI agents are on your Macs</h5>
              <p>Claude, Cursor, Copilot, and every other coding assistant your engineers installed, with the device and the user, not just what ABM deployed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <div purpose="feature-image">
              <img alt="Patching that does not wait for you" src="/images/mdm-software-management-800x600@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="image-caption">
              <h5>Patching that does not wait for you</h5>
              <p>Deploy from a catalog of 1,000+ <a href="/software-catalog">Fleet-maintained apps</a>, the App Store, or your own packages. Fleet installs the update when a device falls out of policy.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <div purpose="feature-image">
              <img alt="Filtering devices by platform in Fleet: macOS, Windows, Linux, ChromeOS, iOS, iPadOS, and Android" src="/images/linux-mdm-built-for-linux-600x450@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="image-caption">
              <h5>Macs, Windows, and Linux in one place</h5>
              <p>One inventory, one set of policies, and one contract for every computer you manage, instead of a second tool for everything that isn’t Apple.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <div purpose="feature-image" class="pre-framed">
              <img alt="iPhones and iPads in the same place as your Macs" src="/images/feature-image-fleet-maintained-software-1056x754@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="image-caption">
              <h5>iPhones and iPads in the same place, not in Intune</h5>
              <p>Enroll iOS and iPadOS in user or supervised mode. Profiles, apps, and Lost Mode sit next to your Macs, so mobile stays with the team that knows Apple.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <div purpose="feature-image">
              <img alt="Live answers about any Mac" src="/images/mdm-visibility-800x450@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="image-caption">
              <h5>Live answers about any Mac</h5>
              <p>Ask which Macs are behind on macOS updates, which have a profile applied, or which are missing an app. You get the answer in seconds from the device itself.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <div purpose="feature-image">
              <img alt="Vulnerabilities on macOS software" src="/images/homepage-vulnerability-management-table-617x440@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="image-caption">
              <h5>Vulnerabilities without another license</h5>
              <p>Installed software is matched against known vulnerability data, including CISA’s Known Exploited Vulnerabilities list. No Jamf Protect required.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

